A cube has 8 vertices, which can be alternately colored black and purple. This forms a bipartition of vertices into two sets (color classes) with no adjacent vertices sharing the same color. For a cube with vertices at coordinates \((x, y, z)\) where \(x, y, z \in \{0, 1\}\) (unit cube), the purple vertices are typically the "odd parity" vertices: \((1,0,0)\), \((0,1,0)\), \((0,0,1)\), and \((1,1,1)\). These vertices have an odd number of 1s in their coordinates.


